#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""devloop-install-issues: the open issues that hold a task or concern a
check class on an install that was missing, read at the start of a run for
whether the tool stands on this machine now, and closed by devloop where it
does.

Since 28 October 2026, decision (5) of the roadmap entry of 27 October 2026
on the tool state read by one program, built in the roadmap entry of 28
October 2026 on the hook on SubagentStart. A handover under a no that the
person declines leaves an issue carrying the command, which blocks the task;
until that day the task went on only once they had run the command and
closed the issue themselves, build-work step 3 point 7 and "The task
itself", and the issues a refusal of auto mode leaves, and the one
setup-checks writes at secrets with nobody there, asked the same of them.
The person's wording of 27 October 2026 says instead: "Installierst du es
spaeter doch, merke ich das beim naechsten Start, schliesse das Issue und
baue die Aufgabe." So at the next start devloop reads, for every such issue,
whether the tool stands on this machine now, and closes the issue itself
where it does, with a comment naming the line it read; a task the issue
held is free again, which the readiness query of build-work then finds, and
a check class the issue concerned goes to setup-checks from the next task,
as build-work says for the issues after a refusal of auto mode. Read is
whether the tool stands for use - on the search path for a command, as
bin/devloop-tool-state --build reads it, in the vendor's folder for a
browser for tests - and not whether the handed-over command installed: the
person may have installed by another way, and a tool the search path finds
is one the work can use, "A command handed over is backed, and its result
is read" in docs/skill-conventions.md. The decision as recorded named
--installed; the entry of 28 October 2026 on the hook on SubagentStart says
why the search path is read. Beside it, since the entry of 28 October 2026
on the handover steps, where the search path does not reach a command's
tool, the folder the issue's command installs into is read with
bin/devloop-tool-state --installed, the command and the tool of the issue:
go install puts a tool into ~/go/bin, which often stands on no search path,
and the person then has installed as the issue asked and gets told nothing
of why the issue stays open. Where that folder line says found, this
program prints it as a line of its own, and the run says that the command
installed the tool into the folder, that the folder is not in their PATH,
that the issue therefore stays open, and the steps that put the folder on
their search path, shared/install-issues-read.md, the wording (11) of that
entry. The issue stays open in that case: the search path alone says
whether the work can use the tool. The person's sentence of 27 October
2026 above stands as the record of that day; since 28 October 2026 the
handover says instead that where they install the tool later, devloop
closes the issue at the next start and builds the task, wording (1) of the
entry on the handover steps.

Usage
    devloop-install-issues [DIR]
    devloop-install-issues --self-test

DIR is the project directory; without it CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR, and without
that the current directory, as bin/devloop-install-record takes it. The
skills run it at every start, before the first thing that reads an open
issue: the readiness query of build-work step 2, and the question on the
rule for the classifier of auto mode in build-work and in plan-work.

Which issues. The open issues of the project labelled raised-here and
needs-human, read with gh issue list, whose body carries the two lines every
issue devloop writes for a missing install carries, shared/install-issue.md,
each at the start of a line of its own:
    install-tool: TOOL
    install-command: COMMAND
TOOL as bin/devloop-tool-state names it - a command as it is called, yq, or
a browser for tests as VENDOR:NAME, playwright:webkit - and COMMAND the
install command as it was handed over, alone, without the change into the
project's directory. An issue without both lines is no issue of this kind
and is passed over; the first line of each name counts where there are
several; the value is read without the spaces around it and without a
carriage return. The kind of each issue: it holds a task where an open
issue is blocked by it, read with gh api
repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/N/dependencies/blocking, the relation the
readiness query reads from the other side, as bin/devloop-auto-mode-question
reads it; it concerns a check class otherwise. gh is found on PATH through
shutil.which, as that program finds it, and called in DIR, each call with 60
seconds.

What is read. For each issue, bin/devloop-tool-state --build TOOL, the
program beside this one run with bash in DIR, whose first line is the
tool's: found, not found, or not read. Found: the issue is closed with gh
issue close N --comment, the comment naming the line:
    devloop closed this issue at the start of a session, the tool standing on this machine now: <the line>
Not found, or not read: nothing is done, and the issue stays open with the
task it holds. A TOOL the program refuses - not a bare word and not
VENDOR:NAME of one of its vendors, which the program alone judges, so that
this one carries no second reading of the form - is read as not read, with
the program's message, and the issue stays: what devloop cannot read it
does not close.

Output, one group per issue in the order of the numbers, each line one key:
    issue: N
    kind: task|class
    title: <the title>
    tool: <the line of bin/devloop-tool-state, or not read, because ...>
    folder: <the first line of bin/devloop-tool-state --installed COMMAND TOOL, where it says found>
    command: COMMAND
    closed: yes|no
The folder line stands only where the tool line says not found on the
search path and the folder line of --installed says found at: the command
installed and the search path does not reach the tool. Where --installed
says not found or not read, or refuses the command's installer, no folder
line stands, there being nothing to tell. Six lines per group, seven with
the folder line. And, where no open issue carries the two lines, one line:
    issues: none of the N open issues labelled raised-here and needs-human carries the lines install-tool and install-command
Exit 0 where every such issue was read, closed or not. Exit 2 where a query
did not answer - gh issue list, the read of the blocking relation, the tool
state program under --build or --installed, or the closing - with the
groups read until then, then
    issues: not decided
    command: <the command that did not answer>
    message: <what came back>
so that the run says so, as "When a command does not answer" asks, and
concludes nothing of the issues not read; an issue closed before that stays
closed, which the comment on it says. Exit 2 with one line on stderr on an
argument it does not know.

It needs python3, bash, git and gh, and nothing else. The self-test runs
every outcome against a stand-in for gh that answers from scripted files, as
the self-test of bin/devloop-auto-mode-question does - the stand-in first on
the path, a launcher of sh on macOS and Linux and of cmd on Windows, the
configuration of gh pointing at an empty directory, no token in the
environment - and against the real bin/devloop-tool-state beside this
program, with a tool that stands on the self-test's own path and one that
does not, one installed by a stand-in for go into a folder the search path
does not reach, and a browser in a project without its package, which the
program cannot read; nothing of it reaches GitHub, and the settings of this
machine are not touched.
"""

import json
import os
import shutil
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
TOOL_STATE = os.path.join(HERE, "devloop-tool-state")
LABELS = ["raised-here", "needs-human"]
SECONDS = 60


def usage(msg):
    print("devloop-install-issues: %s" % msg, file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


def project_dir(given):
    return given or os.environ.get("CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR") or os.getcwd()


def one_line(s):
    return " ".join(s.replace("\r\n", "\n").replace("\r", "\n").split("\n"))


def run(args, d, env=None):
    """The output of a command, or None with the command shown and the
    message. The first word is found on PATH through shutil.which, as a
    shell finds it, and goes through as it is where PATH holds none."""
    shown = " ".join(args)
    exe = shutil.which(args[0]) or args[0]
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([exe] + args[1:], cwd=d, capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=SECONDS, env=env)
    except OSError as e:
        return None, shown, str(e), None
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return None, shown, "no answer within %d seconds" % SECONDS, None
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return None, shown, (r.stderr.strip() or r.stdout.strip() or
                             "exit %d with nothing printed" % r.returncode), r
    return r.stdout, shown, None, r


def field(body, name):
    """The value of the first line of the body that starts with name and a
    colon, without the spaces around it; None where there is none."""
    for line in body.replace("\r\n", "\n").replace("\r", "\n").split("\n"):
        if line.startswith(name + ":"):
            return line[len(name) + 1:].strip()
    return None


def not_decided(command, message):
    print("issues: not decided")
    print("command: %s" % one_line(command))
    print("message: %s" % one_line(message))
    return 2


def tool_line(tool, d):
    """The first line of bin/devloop-tool-state --build TOOL, and whether
    it says found; (line, found, failure) with failure the command and the
    message where the program did not answer."""
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env["CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR"] = d
    out, shown, err, r = run(["bash", TOOL_STATE, "--build", tool], d, env)
    shown = "bin/devloop-tool-state --build %s" % tool
    if r is None:
        return None, False, (shown, err)
    if r.returncode == 2:
        return "not read, because bin/devloop-tool-state refused the tool: %s" % one_line(r.stderr.strip() or err or ""), False, None
    if r.returncode not in (0, 1):
        return None, False, (shown, err or "ended with %d" % r.returncode)
    first = (r.stdout.split("\n") or [""])[0].strip()
    if not first.startswith(tool + ": "):
        return None, False, (shown, "printed no line for %s: %s" % (tool, one_line(r.stdout.strip() or r.stderr.strip())))
    return first, first.startswith(tool + ": found at "), None


def folder_line(tool, command, d):
    """The first line of bin/devloop-tool-state --installed COMMAND TOOL,
    the folder the command's installer writes to, read where the search
    path does not reach the tool, since the entry of 28 October 2026 on the
    handover steps: (line, failure), the line only where it says found at,
    None where it says not found or not read or the program refuses the
    command or the tool, and failure the command and the message where the
    program did not answer."""
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env["CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR"] = d
    out, shown, err, rr = run(["bash", TOOL_STATE, "--installed", command, tool], d, env)
    shown = "bin/devloop-tool-state --installed '%s' %s" % (command, tool)
    if rr is None:  # the folder read: the program did not answer
        return None, (shown, err)
    if rr.returncode == 2:  # the folder read: the command's installer or the tool refused, nothing to tell
        return None, None
    if rr.returncode not in (0, 1):  # the folder read: ended otherwise than the program ends
        return None, (shown, err or "ended with %d" % rr.returncode)
    folder = (rr.stdout.split("\n") or [""])[0].strip()
    if folder.startswith(tool + ": found at "):  # the folder read: the command installed there
        return folder, None
    return None, None


def read(d):
    out, shown, err, _ = run(["gh", "issue", "list", "--state", "open",
                              "--label", LABELS[0], "--label", LABELS[1],
                              "--limit", "100", "--json", "number,title,body"], d)
    if out is None:
        return not_decided(shown, err)
    try:
        issues = json.loads(out)
        if not isinstance(issues, list):
            raise ValueError("not a list")
    except ValueError as e:
        return not_decided(shown, "printed no JSON list of issues: %s" % e)
    found = []
    for issue in sorted((i for i in issues if isinstance(i, dict)),
                        key=lambda i: i.get("number") if isinstance(i.get("number"), int) else 0):
        number = issue.get("number")
        if not isinstance(number, int):
            continue
        body = issue.get("body") or ""
        tool = field(body, "install-tool")
        command = field(body, "install-command")
        if not tool or not command:
            continue
        found.append((number, issue.get("title") or "", tool, command))
    if not found:
        print("issues: none of the %d open issues labelled %s and %s carries the lines "
              "install-tool and install-command" % (len(issues), LABELS[0], LABELS[1]))
        return 0
    for number, title, tool, command in found:
        out, shown, err, _ = run(["gh", "api", "repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/%d/dependencies/blocking" % number], d)
        if out is None:
            return not_decided(shown, err)
        try:
            blocking = json.loads(out)
            if not isinstance(blocking, list):
                raise ValueError("not a list")
        except ValueError as e:
            return not_decided(shown, "printed no JSON list of issues: %s" % e)
        kind = "task" if any(isinstance(b, dict) and b.get("state") == "open"
                             for b in blocking) else "class"
        line, stands, failure = tool_line(tool, d)
        if failure is not None:
            return not_decided(*failure)
        folder = None
        if not stands and line.startswith(tool + ": not found on the search path"):
            folder, failure = folder_line(tool, command, d)
            if failure:  # the folder read did not answer
                return not_decided(*failure)
        print("issue: %d" % number)
        print("kind: %s" % kind)
        print("title: %s" % one_line(title))
        print("tool: %s" % line)
        if folder is not None:
            print("folder: %s" % folder)
        print("command: %s" % one_line(command))
        if not stands:
            print("closed: no")
            continue
        comment = ("devloop closed this issue at the start of a session, the tool standing "
                   "on this machine now: %s" % line)
        out, shown, err, _ = run(["gh", "issue", "close", str(number), "--comment", comment], d)
        if out is None:
            print("closed: no")
            return not_decided(shown, err)
        print("closed: yes")
    return 0


# ------------------------------------------------------------- the self-test

# The stand-in for gh: a Python program behind the launcher the system runs
# off PATH, as in the self-test of bin/devloop-auto-mode-question. It answers
# the issue list, the blocking relation of one issue and the closing of one
# issue from scripted files under $DEVLOOP_ISSUES_SCRIPT, logs every call,
# and marks a call no answer was scripted for.
STAND_IN = r'''import os
import re
import sys
S = os.environ["DEVLOOP_ISSUES_SCRIPT"]
args = " ".join(sys.argv[1:])
with open(os.path.join(S, "log"), "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
    f.write(args + "\n")
m = re.match(r"api repos/\{owner\}/\{repo\}/issues/([0-9]+)/dependencies/blocking$", args)
c = re.match(r"issue close ([0-9]+) --comment ", args)
if args.startswith("issue list "):
    a = os.path.join(S, "issues")
elif m:
    a = os.path.join(S, "blocking." + m.group(1))
elif c:
    a = os.path.join(S, "close." + c.group(1))
else:
    a = os.path.join(S, "other")
if not os.path.exists(a + ".rc"):
    sys.stderr.write("stand-in for gh: no answer scripted for: gh %s\n" % args)
    open(os.path.join(S, "unscripted"), "w").close()
    sys.exit(1)
sys.stdout.write(open(a + ".out", encoding="utf-8").read())
sys.stderr.write(open(a + ".err", encoding="utf-8").read())
sys.exit(int(open(a + ".rc", encoding="utf-8").read().strip()))
'''
LAUNCHER_SH = '#!/bin/sh\nexec "%s" "%s" "$@"\n'
LAUNCHER_CMD = '@"%s" "%%~dp0gh-stand-in.py" %%*\r\n'


def same(a, b):
    return os.path.normcase(os.path.realpath(a)) == os.path.normcase(os.path.realpath(b))


def selftest():
    me = os.path.realpath(__file__)
    T = os.path.realpath(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="devloop-install-issues."))
    n = [0]
    red = [0]
    try:
        for sub in ("bin", "gh-config", "config", "s", "p", "home", os.path.join("home", "bin")):
            os.makedirs(os.path.join(T, sub))
        stand_in = os.path.join(T, "bin", "gh-stand-in.py")
        with open(stand_in, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(STAND_IN)
        if os.name == "nt":
            gh_path = os.path.join(T, "bin", "gh.cmd")
            with open(gh_path, "w", encoding="utf-8", newline="") as f:
                f.write(LAUNCHER_CMD % sys.executable)
        else:
            gh_path = os.path.join(T, "bin", "gh")
            with open(gh_path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                f.write(LAUNCHER_SH % (sys.executable, stand_in))
            os.chmod(gh_path, os.stat(gh_path).st_mode | stat.S_IXUSR | stat.S_IXGRP | stat.S_IXOTH)
        # the tool that stands on the self-test's own path, and no other
        present = os.path.join(T, "home", "bin", "devloop-selftest-present")
        open(present, "w").close()
        os.chmod(present, os.stat(present).st_mode | stat.S_IXUSR | stat.S_IXGRP | stat.S_IXOTH)
        # the stand-in for go, answering go env GOBIN with a folder the search path does not reach, and the tool standing in that folder: an sh script, since bin/devloop-tool-state calls go from bash, on Windows under Git Bash as on the others
        go_bin = os.path.join(T, "home", "go", "bin")
        os.makedirs(go_bin)
        infolder = os.path.join(go_bin, "devloop-selftest-infolder")
        open(infolder, "w").close()
        os.chmod(infolder, os.stat(infolder).st_mode | stat.S_IXUSR | stat.S_IXGRP | stat.S_IXOTH)
        go_path = os.path.join(T, "bin", "go")
        with open(go_path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write("#!/bin/sh\nprintf '%s\\n' '%s'\n" % ("%s", go_bin.replace("\\", "/")))
        os.chmod(go_path, os.stat(go_path).st_mode | stat.S_IXUSR | stat.S_IXGRP | stat.S_IXOTH)
        subprocess.run(["git", "init", "-q", os.path.join(T, "p")], check=True)
        env = dict(os.environ)
        for k in ("GH_TOKEN", "GITHUB_TOKEN", "GH_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN",
                  "GITHUB_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN", "GH_HOST", "CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR"):
            env.pop(k, None)
        env["PATH"] = os.path.join(T, "bin") + os.pathsep + os.path.join(T, "home", "bin") + os.pathsep + env.get("PATH", "")
        env["GH_CONFIG_DIR"] = os.path.join(T, "gh-config")
        env["CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"] = os.path.join(T, "config")
        env["DEVLOOP_ISSUES_SCRIPT"] = os.path.join(T, "s")
        env["HOME"] = os.path.join(T, "home")
        found = shutil.which("gh", path=env["PATH"])
        if found is None or not same(found, gh_path):
            print("FAIL the stand-in for gh is not the gh the path finds: %s" % found)
            return 1
        S = os.path.join(T, "s")

        def fresh():
            for name in os.listdir(S):
                os.unlink(os.path.join(S, name))
            open(os.path.join(S, "log"), "w").close()

        def ans(kind, rc, out, err=""):
            for suffix, content in ((".rc", "%d\n" % rc), (".out", out), (".err", err)):
                with open(os.path.join(S, kind + suffix), "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                    f.write(content)

        def issues(*items):
            ans("issues", 0, json.dumps([{"number": num, "title": title, "body": body}
                                         for num, title, body in items]))

        def blocking(num, *states):
            ans("blocking.%d" % num, 0, json.dumps(
                [{"number": 900 + i, "state": s} for i, s in enumerate(states)]))

        def close(num, rc=0, err=""):
            ans("close.%d" % num, rc, "", err)

        last = {"out": ""}

        def go(*args):
            r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me] + list(args), cwd=os.path.join(T, "p"),
                               capture_output=True, text=True, env=env)
            last["out"] = r.stdout + r.stderr
            return r.returncode, last["out"]

        def got_out(_):
            return last["out"].split("\n")

        def calls():
            with open(os.path.join(S, "log"), encoding="utf-8") as f:
                return [l.rstrip("\n") for l in f if l.strip()]

        def expect(name, got, want_rc, *want_lines, absent=()):
            rc, out = got
            n[0] += 1
            lines = out.split("\n")
            ok = True
            if rc != want_rc:
                print("FAIL %s: exit %d, expected %d" % (name, rc, want_rc))
                ok = False
            for w in want_lines:
                if ok and w not in lines:
                    print("FAIL %s: no line %r" % (name, w))
                    ok = False
            for w in absent:
                if ok and w in lines:
                    print("FAIL %s: the line %r stands there and should not" % (name, w))
                    ok = False
            if ok and os.path.exists(os.path.join(S, "unscripted")):
                print("FAIL %s: the stand-in was called for something no case scripted" % name)
                ok = False
            if not ok:
                print("    " + "\n    ".join(lines))
                red[0] += 1
                return False
            print("ok   %s" % name)
            return True

        def called(name, *want):
            n[0] += 1
            got = calls()
            if got != list(want):
                print("FAIL %s: calls %r, expected %r" % (name, got, list(want)))
                red[0] += 1
                return False
            print("ok   %s" % name)
            return True

        body_present = "The tool is missing.\n\ninstall-tool: devloop-selftest-present\ninstall-command: brew install devloop-selftest-present\n"
        body_absent = "install-tool: devloop-selftest-absent\r\ninstall-command:   brew install devloop-selftest-absent  \r\n"
        comment = "devloop closed this issue at the start of a session, the tool standing on this machine now: "
        present_line = "devloop-selftest-present: found at ~/bin/devloop-selftest-present (on %s)" % __import__("datetime").date.today().isoformat()

        # the arguments
        fresh()
        expect("an option it does not know: 2, one line on stderr", go("--x"), 2,
               "devloop-install-issues: unknown option --x; the one option is --self-test")
        fresh()
        expect("two directories: 2", go("a", "b"), 2,
               "devloop-install-issues: expected at most one directory argument, the project directory, got 2")

        # the listing
        fresh(); ans("issues", 1, "", "gh: Not logged in")
        expect("gh issue list does not answer: not decided, 2", go(), 2,
               "issues: not decided",
               "command: gh issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body",
               "message: gh: Not logged in")
        fresh(); ans("issues", 0, "not json")
        expect("gh issue list prints no JSON list: not decided, 2", go(), 2, "issues: not decided")
        fresh(); issues()
        expect("no open issue: none carries the lines, 0", go(), 0,
               "issues: none of the 0 open issues labelled raised-here and needs-human carries the lines install-tool and install-command")
        fresh(); issues((3, "A finding", "no lines here"), (4, "Install yq", "install-tool: yq\nno command line"))
        expect("issues without both lines: passed over, 0", go(), 0,
               "issues: none of the 2 open issues labelled raised-here and needs-human carries the lines install-tool and install-command")
        called("issues without both lines: the blocking relation of none read",
               "issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body")

        # the tool standing: closed with the comment
        fresh(); issues((7, "Install devloop-selftest-present for task #5", body_present)); blocking(7, "open"); close(7)
        expect("the tool stands on the search path: closed, the task's issue, 0", go(), 0,
               "issue: 7", "kind: task", "title: Install devloop-selftest-present for task #5",
               "tool: " + present_line, "command: brew install devloop-selftest-present", "closed: yes")
        called("the tool stands: the listing, the blocking relation, the closing with the comment naming the line",
               "issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body",
               "api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/7/dependencies/blocking",
               "issue close 7 --comment " + comment + present_line)
        # the tool not standing: nothing done
        fresh(); issues((8, "Install devloop-selftest-absent", body_absent)); blocking(8)
        expect("the tool does not stand: not closed, the class's issue, the values trimmed, 0", go(), 0,
               "issue: 8", "kind: class",
               "tool: devloop-selftest-absent: not found on the search path (on %s)" % present_line[-11:-1],
               "command: brew install devloop-selftest-absent", "closed: no")
        called("the tool does not stand: no closing called",
               "issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body",
               "api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/8/dependencies/blocking")
        n[0] += 1
        if any(l.startswith("folder:") for l in got_out(go)):
            print("FAIL the tool does not stand and brew's folder is not read: a folder line stands there and should not"); red[0] += 1
        else:
            print("ok   the tool does not stand and brew's folder is not read: no folder line")
        # the tool installed by go into a folder the search path does not reach: not closed, the folder line
        fresh(); issues((17, "Install devloop-selftest-infolder for task #6", "install-tool: devloop-selftest-infolder\ninstall-command: go install example.invalid/x/cmd/devloop-selftest-infolder@latest\n")); blocking(17, "open")
        expect("the command installed into a folder the search path does not reach: not closed, the folder line, 0", go(), 0,
               "issue: 17", "kind: task",
               "tool: devloop-selftest-infolder: not found on the search path (on %s)" % present_line[-11:-1],
               "command: go install example.invalid/x/cmd/devloop-selftest-infolder@latest", "closed: no")
        # the folder line read by its head and its tail: the folder stands between them as bash writes it on this system, ~/go/bin where the home directory is read as such
        n[0] += 1
        head = "folder: devloop-selftest-infolder: found at "
        tail = "go/bin/devloop-selftest-infolder, the folder go writes to, read off go env GOBIN (on %s)" % present_line[-11:-1]
        if any(l.startswith(head) and l.endswith(tail) for l in got_out(go)):
            print("ok   the folder line names the folder go writes to, read off go env GOBIN")
        else:
            print("FAIL the folder line names the folder go writes to, read off go env GOBIN: no line from %r to %r" % (head, tail)); red[0] += 1
            print("    " + "\n    ".join(got_out(go)))
        called("the folder read: no closing called",
               "issue list --state open --label raised-here --label needs-human --limit 100 --json number,title,body",
               "api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/17/dependencies/blocking")
        # a browser the program cannot read: not closed
        fresh(); issues((9, "Download WebKit", "install-tool: playwright:webkit\ninstall-command: npx playwright install webkit\n")); blocking(9, "closed", "open")
        expect("a browser not read: not closed, the cause, 0", go(), 0,
               "issue: 9", "kind: task",
               "tool: playwright:webkit: not read, because the project has no Playwright package to say which version of webkit it needs, no node_modules/.bin/playwright (on %s)" % present_line[-11:-1],
               "closed: no")
        # a tool in no form the program reads
        fresh(); issues((10, "Install", "install-tool: a b\ninstall-command: x\n")); blocking(10)
        expect("a tool in no form the program reads: not read with its message, not closed, 0", go(), 0,
               "issue: 10", "tool: not read, because bin/devloop-tool-state refused the tool: devloop-tool-state: a TOOL is a bare word as it is called - letters, digits, dot, hyphen, plus and underscore - or VENDOR:NAME for a browser, got 'a b'",
               "closed: no")
        fresh(); issues((11, "Install", "install-tool: chrome:webkit\ninstall-command: x\n")); blocking(11)
        expect("a browser of a vendor the program refuses: not read with its message, not closed, 0", go(), 0,
               "issue: 11", "tool: not read, because bin/devloop-tool-state refused the tool: devloop-tool-state: a browser is named VENDOR:NAME with playwright, puppeteer or cypress as the vendor, and cypress:binary for Cypress, got 'chrome:webkit'",
               "closed: no")
        # several, in the order of the numbers, one closed and one not
        fresh(); issues((20, "Second", body_absent), (12, "First", body_present)); blocking(12, "open"); blocking(20, "open"); close(12)
        got = go()
        expect("several issues: in the order of the numbers, one closed, one not, 0", got, 0,
               "issue: 12", "closed: yes", "issue: 20", "closed: no")
        n[0] += 1
        out_lines = got[1].split("\n")
        if out_lines.index("issue: 12") < out_lines.index("issue: 20"):
            print("ok   several issues: the lower number first")
        else:
            print("FAIL several issues: the lower number first"); red[0] += 1
        # queries that do not answer
        fresh(); issues((13, "T", body_present)); ans("blocking.13", 1, "", "gh: HTTP 404")
        expect("the blocking relation does not answer: not decided, 2", go(), 2,
               "issues: not decided", "command: gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/13/dependencies/blocking", "message: gh: HTTP 404")
        fresh(); issues((14, "T", body_present)); blocking(14, "open"); close(14, 1, "gh: HTTP 403")
        expect("the closing does not answer: the group with closed: no, then not decided, 2", go(), 2,
               "issue: 14", "closed: no", "issues: not decided", "command: gh issue close 14 --comment " + comment + present_line, "message: gh: HTTP 403")
        fresh(); issues((15, "First", body_present), (16, "Second", body_present)); blocking(15, "open"); close(15); ans("blocking.16", 1, "", "gh: HTTP 500")
        expect("a query fails after an issue was closed: that issue's group stands, then not decided, 2", go(), 2,
               "issue: 15", "closed: yes", "issues: not decided", "message: gh: HTTP 500", absent=("issue: 16",))
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(T, ignore_errors=True)
    if red[0]:
        print("SELF-TEST FAILED: %d of %d cases red" % (red[0], n[0]))
        return 1
    print("SELF-TEST PASSED: %d cases" % n[0])
    return 0


def main(argv):
    if argv == ["--self-test"]:
        return selftest()
    given = None
    dirs = 0
    for a in argv:
        if a.startswith("-"):
            return usage("unknown option %s; the one option is --self-test" % a)
        dirs += 1
        given = a
    if dirs > 1:
        return usage("expected at most one directory argument, the project directory, got %d" % dirs)
    return read(project_dir(given))


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
